{"name":"Hyacinth Mauve","hex":"#6f729f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hyacinth","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any bulbous plant of the genus Hyacinthus, native to the Mediterranean and South Africa.","A variety of zircon, ranging in color from brown, orange, reddish-brown and yellow; a jacinth."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hyacinth"},{"term":"mauve","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rich purple synthetic dye, which faded easily, briefly popular c. 1859‒1873 and now called mauveine.","A pale purple or violet colour, like the colour of the dye after it has faded."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a pale purple colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mauve"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hyacinth","blocks":[{"text":"Hyacinthus is a genus of bulbous herbs and spring-blooming perennials. They are fragrant flowering plants in the family Asparagaceae, subfamily Scilloideae and are commonly called hyacinths. The genus is native predominantly to the Eastern Mediterranean region from the south of Turkey to the Palestine region, although naturalized more widely."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Hyacinth_-_Anglesey_Abbey.jpg/330px-Hyacinth_-_Anglesey_Abbey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyacinth","wikidata":"Q158758"},{"title":"Mauve","blocks":[{"text":"Mauve is a pale purple color named after the mallow flower. The first use of the word mauve as a color was in 1796–1798 according to the Oxford English Dictionary, but its use seems to have been rare before 1859. Another name for the color is mallow, with the first recorded use of mallow as a color name in English in 1611."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Wilde_Malve.JPG/330px-Wilde_Malve.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.w